  "prediction": "**United Airlines Introduces New Boarding Process and Expands Latin American Routes**\n\nUnited Airlines has made significant changes to its boarding process and expanded its routes in Latin America. The airline has introduced a new two-lane, color-coded boarding process that aims to reduce congestion and make the boarding process more efficient. The new system, which has been tested at Los Angeles International Airport since February, will now be rolled out to all United locations.\n\nUnder the new system, passengers will be divided into five groups, with elite and priority passengers boarding through lane 1 and the remaining passengers boarding through lane 2. The lanes will be numbered \"1\" and \"2,\" with lane 1 being the \"blue\" lane and lane 2 being the \"green\" lane. Pre-boarding will be open to top-tier \"Premier 1K\" and \"Global Services\" frequent-flyers, active military, customers with disabilities, and families with children ages 2 and younger.\n\nThe new boarding process is designed to reduce congestion and make the boarding process more efficient. United has also introduced push notifications for passengers who have the airline's mobile app, which will alert them when boarding starts. This feature is expected to be particularly useful for passengers who experience flight delays or early boarding.\n\nIn addition to the new boarding process, United has also expanded its routes in Latin America through a joint business agreement (JBA) with Compañía Panameña de Aviación S.A. (Copa) and Aerovías del Continente Americano S.A. (Avianca). The JBA, which is pending government approval, is expected to provide substantial benefits for customers, communities, and the marketplace for air travel between the United States and 19 countries in Central and South America.\n\nThe JBA is expected to drive significant traffic growth at major gateway cities coast to coast, which will help bring new investment and create more economic development opportunities. The agreement will also enable United, Copa, and Avianca to compete more effectively, offer competitive fares, and increase service, encouraging innovation and establishing a more robust and vibrant marketplace.\n\nOverall, United's new boarding process and expanded Latin American routes are expected to provide a better experience for passengers and drive growth for the airline.",
